+*   Use when computational resources are limited [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-NeILF was introduced in the context of 3D Gaussian Splatting to enable inverse rendering by decomposing features into physically based components [S1, unofficial]. It builds on the principles of Gaussian Splatting and extends them with a Blinn-Phong lighting model and specialized loss functions for training [S1, unofficial].
+
+### Origin
+*   NeILF was introduced in the context of Gaussian Splatting extensions for inverse rendering [S1, unofficial].
+*   The method decomposes Gaussian Splatting features into diffuse, specular, and ambient components using a Blinn-Phong model [S1, unofficial].
+
+### History and Development
+*   Developed as part of the VEG project, which explores various aspects of Gaussian Splatting [S1, unofficial].
+*   The method supports multi-Gaussian rendering and palette-based color offsets [S1, unofficial].
+
+### Where it is Used
+*   Used in applications requiring inverse rendering and material optimization, such as 3D reconstruction and relighting [S1, unofficial].
 
